+
+### GPU-based missing brick reporting via hash table
+A subsystem for recording missing bricks identified during ray-casting on the GPU, using a lock-free hash table stored in an image load store buffer to coordinate disk access and update the volume pool [S2]. It uses the `GL_ARB_shader_image_load_store` extension (core in OpenGL 4.2) to create an image buffer independent of the current rendering buffer [S2]. It implements a set-based linearly-probed lock-free hash table using atomic load/store operations [S2]. Hashing is based on the brick identifier so that multiple rays requesting the same brick hash to the same position, keeping the table size small and avoiding duplicate values inherent in multiple render target approaches [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The per-frame cost is dominated by the ray casting itself. The overhead of brick identification is minimal as it parallelizes effectively on the GPU [S2]. The hash table operations are $O(1)$ on average due to the lock-free linear probing design [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The working set is minimized to only the bricks accessed during rendering [S1, unofficial]. The hash table size is proportional to the number of unique missing bricks per frame, which is kept small by hashing on brick IDs and exploiting spatial coherency [S2].
-*   **Performance:** The method scales to extremely large datasets by streaming data on demand [S2]. It avoids the conservative memory estimates of earlier approaches [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Optimal accuracy (never loads unused data), efficient GPU utilization, handles large datasets out-of-core [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires multiple frames to converge to full resolution for regions with missing data [S2]. The "Greedy" strategy may generate more hash table entries than necessary, while the "Global" strategy may have slower visual convergence [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Large-scale volume rendering where the entire dataset cannot fit in GPU memory and interactive or near-interactive frame rates are required [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Small datasets that fit entirely in GPU memory, or applications requiring single-frame full-resolution accuracy without iterative refinement [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The per-frame cost is dominated by ray casting and hash table insertions. Hash table operations are $O(1)$ amortized due to linear probing and atomic operations [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the number of resident bricks in the cache plus the size of the hash table [S2]. The hash table size is kept small by hashing on brick IDs, which exploits spatial coherency (neighboring rays request similar bricks) [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** The greedy strategy was found to converge in the fewest number of frames and produce more pleasing progress information, despite flooding the hash table, because it enables earlier ray termination [S2]. The global strategy generates the final correct image quickest in raw completion time but offers less responsive intermediate progress [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Minimizes working-set size by only streaming accessed bricks [S1, unofficial]. Ensures optimal accuracy by never loading unused data [S2]. Scales to extremely large data sets [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires complex GPU-side logic for missing brick reporting and hash table management [S2]. Previous approaches using conservative working-set estimates led to sub-optimal memory utilization [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Large-scale volume rendering where data exceeds GPU memory capacity and interactive or progressive refinement is desired [S1, unofficial][S2].
+*   **When not to use:** When data fits entirely in GPU memory, or when the overhead of hash table management and frame-to-frame resumption is unacceptable compared to simpler in-core methods.
 
 ## Background
-Ray-guided volume rendering was introduced as a scalable approach to handle large biomedicinal and scientific datasets [S1, unofficial][S2]. Early approaches produced conservative estimates of the working set, leading to sub-optimal memory utilization [S1, unofficial]. The specific method using missing brick reporting with a GPU-based hash table was developed to overcome the limitations of multiple render targets, which were limited in number and ignored spatial coherency [S2]. The technique leverages OpenGL 4.2 features (`GL_ARB_shader_image_load_store`) to implement efficient, lock-free data structures on the GPU [S2]. It represents a shift from pre-computing access patterns to dynamic, ray-driven data streaming [S1, unofficial].
+Ray-guided volume rendering was introduced to address the limitations of early approaches that produced conservative estimates of the working set, leading to sub-optimal memory utilization [S1, unofficial]. Key early works include [CNLE09], [HBJP12], [FSK13], and [BPH14] [S1, unofficial]. The specific technique of missing brick reporting with GPU-based hash table coordination was detailed by Crassin et al. [S2] (PMC4260263). The method builds on the principle of address translation using octrees or page-table hierarchies [S1, unofficial]. The use of `GL_ARB_shader_image_load_store` for lock-free hash tables was adopted to overcome the limitations of multiple render targets, which ignore spatial coherency and lead to duplicate values and larger texture reads back [S2].
 
